\section{HTML in cells}{

By default all cell values and text (titles, labels, footnotes, ...) are
HTML-escaped, so special characters like \code{<}, \code{>}, and \code{&} render as
literal text. To emit content as raw HTML instead:
\itemize{
\item For body cells, mark whole columns with \code{\link[=lt_html]{lt_html()}}.
\item For a title, subtitle, column label, spanner, footnote, or note, wrap the
text in \code{\link[=I]{I()}}. For example, \code{lt_header(x, I("<b>Report</b>"))} renders the
title as raw HTML, while \code{lt_header(x, "<b>Report</b>")} shows the literal
\verb{<b>} tags.
}
}

\section{Interactivity}{

When a cell value has been formatted (e.g., by auto-formatting or
\code{\link[=lt_format]{lt_format()}}), the original value is stored in the cell's \code{title}
attribute and shown as a tooltip on hover. Additionally:
\itemize{
\item \code{Alt + Click} on a table toggles display of all raw values in that table.
\item \code{Alt + Double-Click} on a table toggles raw values globally (all tables
on the page).
}

Raw values are shown as \code{(value)} after the formatted text, highlighted
with a light yellow background.
}

\section{Missing values}{

By default, missing (\code{NA}) cells display an em dash (\verb{—}) so they are not
confused with empty strings. Change the table-wide default with the
\code{lt.missing} global option, e.g. \code{options(lt.missing = "n/a")}; set it to
the empty string (\code{options(lt.missing = "")}) to leave \code{NA} cells blank.
Use \code{\link[=lt_sub]{lt_sub()}} with \verb{missing =} to override the text for specific columns.
}

\section{Repeated data in \code{litedown::fuse()} documents}{

A document often shows several tables built from the same data, e.g., one
data frame with different columns, groups, or formatting in each table.
To keep the output file small, \code{lt()} writes the data into the document only
once; the other tables just point to that copy instead of repeating the
data. This happens automatically when the document is rendered by
\code{\link[litedown:fuse]{litedown::fuse()}}, including documents that include child documents.

Sharing saves space except for tiny data: a data frame with roughly 15 cells
or fewer (e.g., 5 rows by 3 columns) is about as small as the pointer to it,
so sharing it gains nothing and may add a few bytes. For anything larger, the
saving grows with the size of the data and the number of tables using it.
To share only data frames above a certain size, set the \code{lt.share_size}
global option (in bytes, as reported by \code{\link[utils:object.size]{utils::object.size()}}), e.g.,
\code{options(lt.share_size = 1e4)}; smaller data is then written into each
table. Use \code{options(lt.share_size = Inf)} to turn sharing off.
}

